<H1 class=3DTitle><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>2<BR><A=20
name=3DPFGRF002>Optimizer =
Operations</A></FONT></H1><!--/TOC=3DTitle--><A=20
name=3D1006797></A>
<P class=3DBP>This chapter expands on the ideas introduced in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#721">Chapter&nbsp;1,=20
"Introduction to the Optimizer"</A> and explains optimizer actions in =
greater=20
detail for specific cases. This chapter describes how the cost-based =
optimizer=20
evaluates expressions and performs specific operations. It also explains =
how the=20
CBO transforms some SQL statements into others to achieve the same goal =
more=20
efficiently.</P><A name=3D1006498></A>
<P class=3DBP>The chapter contains the following sections:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1006468></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004574">How=20
  the Optimizer Performs Operations</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1006472></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005536">How=20
  the Optimizer Transforms SQL Statements</A> </LI></UL><A =
name=3D1004574></A><!--TOC=3Dh1-"1004574"-->
<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the=20
Optimizer Performs Operations</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A =
name=3D1004576></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer fully evaluates expressions whenever =
possible and=20
translates certain syntactic constructs into equivalent constructs. =
Constructs=20
are translated in the following cases:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1008286></A>Oracle&nbsp;can more =
quickly=20
  evaluate the resulting expression than the original expression.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1008294></A>The original =
expression is a=20
  syntactic equivalent of the resulting expression. Sometimes, different =
SQL=20
  constructs can operate identically. For example, Oracle maps =3D=20
  <CODE>ANY</CODE> (<EM class=3DItalic>subquery</EM>) and =
<CODE>IN</CODE> (<EM=20
  class=3DItalic>subquery</EM>) to a single construct. </LI></UL><A=20
name=3D1004577></A>
<P class=3DBP>This section contains the following discussions:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004581></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004651">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates IN-List Iterators</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004585></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004763">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates Concatenation</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004589></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004878">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates Remote Operations</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004593></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004993">How=20
  the CBO Executes Distributed Statements</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004597></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005014">How=20
  the CBO Executes Sort Operations</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004601></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005152">How=20
  the CBO Executes Views</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004605></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005216">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates Constants</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004609></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005234">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the UNION and UNION ALL Operators</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004613></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005289">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the LIKE Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004617></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005298">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the IN Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004621></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005317">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the ANY or SOME Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004625></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005334">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the ALL Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004629></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005356">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the BETWEEN Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004633></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005363">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates the NOT Operator</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004637></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005377">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates Transitivity</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004641></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005424">How=20
  the CBO Optimizes Common Subexpressions</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004645></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005493">How=20
  the CBO Evaluates DETERMINISTIC Functions</A> </LI></UL><A =
name=3D1004651></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1004651"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates IN-List Iterators</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1004652></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>IN</CODE>-list iterator is used when a query =
contains an=20
<CODE>IN</CODE> clause with values. The execution plan is identical to =
what=20
would result for a statement with an equality clause instead of =
<CODE>IN</CODE>=20
except for one additional step. That extra step occurs when the=20
<CODE>IN</CODE>-list iterator feeds the equality clause with unique =
values from=20
the <CODE>IN</CODE>-list.</P><A name=3D1012612></A>
<P class=3DBP>Both of the statements in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004654">Example&nbsp;2-1</A>=20
and <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004654">Example&nbsp;2-1</A>=20
are equivalent and produce the same plan.</P><A name=3D1004654></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-1 IN-List=20
Iterators Initial Statement</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004656></A>SELECT header_id, line_id, revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004657></A>  FROM so_lines_all
<A name=3D1004658></A> WHERE header_id IN (1011,1012,1013);
<A name=3D1004659></A>
<A name=3D1004660></A>SELECT header_id, line_id, revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004661></A>  FROM so_lines_all
<A name=3D1004662></A> WHERE header_id =3D 1011
<A name=3D1004663></A>    OR header_id =3D 1012
<A name=3D1004664></A>    OR header_id =3D 1013;
<A name=3D1004665></A>
<A name=3D1004666></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004667></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004668></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004669></A> INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004670></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004671></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1012656></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1008560></A>
<P class=3DBP>An equivalent statement in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004654">Example&nbsp;2-1</A>=20
binds <CODE>:b1</CODE> to the different unique values by the=20
<CODE>IN</CODE>-list iterator.</P><A name=3D1012653></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-2 Alternate=20
Statement with Equality</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1008557></A>SELECT header_id, line_id, revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004675></A>  FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004676></A> WHERE header_id =3D :b1;
<A name=3D1004677></A>
<A name=3D1004678></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004679></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004680></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004681></A> TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004682></A>  INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1004683></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004684></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008577">Example&nbsp;2-3</A>=20
uses a unique index. Because there is a sort involved on the=20
<CODE>IN</CODE>-list, even with complete keys of unique indexes, there =
is still=20
a range scan.</P><A name=3D1008577></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-3 IN-List=20
Iterator with Unique Index</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004685></A>SELECT header_id, line_id, revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004686></A>  FROM so_lines_all
<A name=3D1004687></A> WHERE line_id IN (1011,1012,1013);
<A name=3D1004688></A>
<A name=3D1004689></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004690></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004691></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004692></A> INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004693></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004694></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_U1
<A name=3D1004695></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004696></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008585">Example&nbsp;2-4</A>,=20
the IN-list operator can be used when driving into a table with a nested =
loop=20
operation.</P><A name=3D1008585></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-4 IN-List=20
Iterator with a Nested Loop</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004697></A>SELECT h.header_id, l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004698></A>  FROM so_headers_all h, so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004699></A> WHERE l.inventory_item_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004700></A>   AND h.order_number =3D l.header_id
<A name=3D1004701></A>   AND h.order_type_id IN (1,2,3);
<A name=3D1004702></A>
<A name=3D1004703></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004704></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004705></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004706></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004707></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004708></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N5
<A name=3D1004709></A>  INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004710></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004711></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_U2
<A name=3D1004712></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004713></A>
<P class=3DBP>The <CODE>IN</CODE>-list operator is especially useful if =
there is=20
an expensive first step that you do not want to repeat for every=20
<CODE>IN</CODE>-list element. In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008601">Example&nbsp;2-5</A>,=20
even though there are three <CODE>IN</CODE>-list elements, the full scan =
on=20
<CODE>so_lines_all</CODE> happens only once.</P><A name=3D1008601></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-5 Using the=20
IN-List Iterator to Avoid an Expensive First Step</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004714></A>SELECT h.header_id, l.line_id, =
l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004715></A>  FROM so_headers_all h, so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004716></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004717></A>   AND h.order_number =3D l.header_id
<A name=3D1004718></A>   AND h.order_type_id IN (1,2,3);
<A name=3D1004719></A>
<A name=3D1004720></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004721></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004722></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004723></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004724></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004725></A>  INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004726></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004727></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_U2
</PRE><A name=3D1004728></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1004728"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>When the=20
Optimizer Uses IN-List Iterators</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D1004729></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer uses an <CODE>IN</CODE>-list iterator when =
an=20
<CODE>IN</CODE> clause is specified with values, and the optimizer finds =
a=20
selective index for that column. If there are multiple <CODE>OR</CODE> =
clauses=20
using the same index, then the optimizer chooses this operation rather =
than=20
<CODE>CONCATENATION</CODE> or <CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE>, =
because it is=20
more efficient.</P><A name=3D1004730></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1004730"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>IN-List=20
Iterator Hints</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1004731></A>
<P class=3DBP>There are no hints for this operation. You can provide a =
hint to use=20
the relevant index, which can cause this operation. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008608">Example&nbsp;2-6</A>=20
shows a query without an <CODE>INDEX</CODE> hint, and the resulting =
execution=20
plan.</P><A name=3D1008608></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-6 Calling=20
the IN-List Iterator without the INDEX Hint</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004733></A>SELECT h.customer_id, l.line_id, =
l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004734></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004735></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D 20
<A name=3D1004736></A>   AND h.original_system_reference =3D =
l.attribute5
<A name=3D1004737></A>   AND h.original_system_source_code IN =
(1013,1014);
<A name=3D1004738></A>
<A name=3D1004739></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004740></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004741></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004742></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004743></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004744></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004745></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N5
<A name=3D1004746></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1012683></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1012672">Example&nbsp;2-7</A>=20
shows a query with an <CODE>INDEX</CODE> hint, and the resulting =
execution=20
plan.</P><A name=3D1012672></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-7 Calling=20
the IN-List Iterator with the INDEX Hint</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004748></A>SELECT /*+INDEX(h so_headers_n9 */ =
h.customer_id, l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004749></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004750></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D 20
<A name=3D1004751></A>   AND h.original_system_reference =3D =
l.attribute5
<A name=3D1004752></A>   AND h.original_system_source_code IN =
(1013,1014);
<A name=3D1004753></A>
<A name=3D1004754></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004755></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004756></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004757></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004758></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004759></A>  INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004760></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004761></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N9
</PRE><A name=3D1004763></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1004763"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates Concatenation</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1008634></A>
<P class=3DBP>Concatenation is useful for statements with different =
conditions=20
combined with an <CODE>OR</CODE> clause. With concatenation, you get a =
good=20
execution plan with appropriate indexes. Examples <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004765">2-8</A>=20
and <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010053">2-9</A>=20
show two plans, each accessing the table through the appropriate index =
and=20
combined using concatenation.</P><A name=3D1010009></A>
<P class=3DBP>The plan in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004765">Example&nbsp;2-8</A>=20
does not return duplicate rows, so for each component it appends a =
negation of=20
the previous components.</P><A name=3D1004765></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-8 How the=20
CBO Evaluates Concatenation</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004767></A>SELECT l.header_id, l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004768></A>  FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004769></A> WHERE l.parent_line_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004770></A>    OR l.service_parent_line_id =3D :b1;
<A name=3D1004771></A>
<A name=3D1004772></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004773></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004774></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004775></A> CONCATENATION
<A name=3D1004776></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004777></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N20
<A name=3D1004778></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004779></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N17
<A name=3D1004780></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004781></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004765">Example&nbsp;2-8</A>,=20
the components are the following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D1004782></A><CODE>l.parent_line_id =3D=20
  :b1</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D1010047></A><CODE>l.service_parent_line_id =3D=20
  :b1</CODE> and <CODE>l.parent_line_id !=3D :b1</CODE> </LI></UL><A=20
name=3D1010048></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010053">Example&nbsp;2-9</A>=20
shows how the optimizer uses concatenation to optimize a nested =
<CODE>OR</CODE>=20
statement.</P><A name=3D1010053></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-9=20
Concatenation of a Query with a Nested OR Statement</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1010054></A>SELECT p.header_id, l.line_id, =
l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004786></A>  FROM so_lines_all p , so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004787></A> WHERE p.header_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004788></A>   AND (l.parent_line_id =3D p.line_id
<A name=3D1004789></A>       OR l.service_parent_line_id =3D p.line_id);
<A name=3D1004790></A>
<A name=3D1004791></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004792></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004793></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004794></A> CONCATENATION
<A name=3D1004795></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004796></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004797></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1004798></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004799></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N20
<A name=3D1004800></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004801></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004802></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1004803></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004804></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N17
<A name=3D1004805></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004806></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010086">Example&nbsp;2-10</A>=20
shows the resulting execution plan for the same query shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010053">Example&nbsp;2-9</A>,=20
but with concatenation specifically disabled by the =
<CODE>NO_EXPAND</CODE>=20
hint.</P><A name=3D1010086></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-10 Query=20
with Nested OR with Concatenation Disabled</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004807></A>SELECT /*+NO_EXPAND */ p.header_id, =
l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004808></A>  FROM so_lines_all p, so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004809></A> WHERE p.header_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004810></A>   AND (l.parent_line_id =3D p.line_id
<A name=3D1004811></A>       OR l.service_parent_line_id =3D p.line_id);
<A name=3D1006403></A>
<A name=3D1006404></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004814></A>-------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004815></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004816></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004817></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004818></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1004819></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1010119></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010116></A>
<P class=3DBP>Trying to execute the statement in a single query produces =
a poor=20
execution plan. Because the optimizer has two paths to follow and has =
been=20
instructed not to decompose the query, it needs to access all the rows =
in the=20
second table to see if any rows match one of the conditions.</P><A=20
name=3D1004820></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1004820"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"=20
color=3D#330099>Concatenation Hints</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D1004821></A>
<P class=3DBP>Use the hint <CODE>USE_CONCAT</CODE> for this =
operation.</P><A=20
name=3D1004822></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1004822"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>When Not to=20
Use Concatenation</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1008349></A>
<P class=3DBP>Concatenation is expensive in the following cases and =
should not be=20
used:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004823></A>When <CODE>OR</CODE> =
conditions=20
  are on same column and can use the <CODE>IN</CODE>-list operator, =
which is=20
  more efficient than concatenation=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1004824></A>If an expensive step =
gets repeated=20
  for every concatenation </LI></UL><A name=3D1004825></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008362">Example&nbsp;2-11</A>=20
illustrates this point.</P><A name=3D1008362></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-11 When Not=20
to Use Concatenation</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1008426></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider the following statement:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004828></A>SELECT h.customer_id, l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004829></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004830></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D 20
<A name=3D1004831></A>   AND h.original_system_reference =3D =
l.attribute5
<A name=3D1004832></A>   AND h.original_system_source_code IN =
(1013,1014);
<A name=3D1004833></A>
<A name=3D1004834></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004835></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004836></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004837></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004838></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004839></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004840></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N5
<A name=3D1004841></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1008385></A>
<P class=3DBP>The initial statement calls for a full scan on=20
<CODE>so_lines_all</CODE> as the first stage. The optimizer chooses to =
use a=20
single column index for the second table, but we want it to use a =
two-column=20
index.</P><A name=3D1004842></A>
<P class=3DBP>You could use a hint to force concatenation as in shown in =
<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1012701">Example&nbsp;2-12</A>,=20
but the initial full scan is still repeated which is not =
desirable.</P><A=20
name=3D1012701></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-12 Using=20
the Concatenation Hint</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1006407></A>SELECT /*+USE_CONCAT*/ h.customer_id, l.line_id, =
l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004844></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004845></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D 20
<A name=3D1004846></A>   AND h.original_system_reference =3D =
l.attribute5
<A name=3D1004847></A>   AND h.original_system_source_code IN =
(1013,1014);
<A name=3D1004848></A>
<A name=3D1004849></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004850></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004851></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004852></A> CONCATENATION
<A name=3D1004853></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004854></A>   TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004855></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004856></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N9
<A name=3D1004857></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004858></A>   TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004859></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004860></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N9
<A name=3D1004861></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1004862></A>
<P class=3DBP>If, instead, you provide a hint to use the two-column =
index, then=20
the optimizer switches to that with an <CODE>IN</CODE>-list iterator. =
The=20
initial scan is not repeated, and a better execution plan results, as=20
follows:</P><A name=3D1012712></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-13 Using=20
the Index Hint</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004863></A>SELECT /*+INDEX(h so_headers_n9 */ h.customer_id, =
l.line_id, l.revenue_amount
<A name=3D1004864></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004865></A> WHERE l.s7 =3D 20
<A name=3D1004866></A>   AND h.original_system_reference =3D =
l.attribute5
<A name=3D1004867></A>   AND h.original_system_source_code IN =
(1013,1014);
<A name=3D1004868></A>
<A name=3D1004869></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004870></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004871></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004872></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004873></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004874></A>  INLIST ITERATOR
<A name=3D1004875></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004876></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N9
</PRE><A name=3D1004878></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1004878"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates Remote Operations</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1008451></A>
<P class=3DBP>The remote operation indicates that there is a table from =
another=20
database being accessed through a database link. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004880">Example&nbsp;2-14</A>=20
has a remote driving table:</P><A name=3D1004880></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-14 How the=20
CBO Evaluates a Query with a Remote Driving Table</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1006410></A>SELECT c.customer_name, count(*)
<A name=3D1004883></A>  FROM ra_customers c, so_headers_all@oe h
<A name=3D1004884></A> WHERE c.customer_id =3D h.customer_id
<A name=3D1004885></A>   AND h.order_number =3D :b1
<A name=3D1004886></A>GROUP BY c.customer_name;
<A name=3D1004887></A>
<A name=3D1004888></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004889></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004890></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004891></A> SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1004892></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004893></A>   REMOTE
<A name=3D1004894></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1004895></A>    INDEX UNIQUE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_U1
</PRE>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1012748></A><STRONG>Remote Database Query Obtained from the =
Library=20
  Cache</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1004898></A>SELECT =
"ORDER_NUMBER","CUSTOMER_ID"
<A name=3D1004899></A>  FROM "SO_HEADERS_ALL" "H"
<A name=3D1004900></A> WHERE "ORDER_NUMBER"=3D:"SYS_B_0";
<A name=3D1004901></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1004902></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004906">Example&nbsp;2-15</A>=20
has a local driving table.</P><A name=3D1004906></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-15 How the=20
CBO Evaluates a Query with a Local Driving Table</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004907></A>SELECT c.customer_name, h.order_number
<A name=3D1004908></A>  FROM ra_customers c, so_headers_all@oe h
<A name=3D1004909></A> WHERE c.customer_id =3D h.customer_id
<A name=3D1004910></A>   AND c.customer_name LIKE :b1;
<A name=3D1006415></A>
<A name=3D1006416></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004913></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004914></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004915></A> MERGE JOIN
<A name=3D1004916></A>  REMOTE
<A name=3D1004917></A>  SORT JOIN
<A name=3D1004918></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1004919></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_N1
</PRE>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1008525></A><STRONG>Remote Database Query Obtained from the =
Library=20
  Cache</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1004922></A>SELECT =
"ORDER_NUMBER","CUSTOMER_ID"
<A name=3D1004923></A>  FROM "SO_HEADERS_ALL" "H"
<A name=3D1004924></A> WHERE "CUSTOMER_ID" IS NOT NULL =20
<A name=3D1004925></A>ORDER BY "CUSTOMER_ID";
<A name=3D1004926></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1008512></A>
<P class=3DBP>A couple of factors influence the execution plan:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1008513></A>Network round trips =
can be several=20
  orders of magnitude more expensive than the physical and logical I/Os. =

  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1008514></A>The optimizer does =
not have any=20
  statistics on the remote database, which might not be an Oracle =
database.=20
</LI></UL><A name=3D1004927></A>
<P class=3DBP>In general, the optimizer chooses to access the remote =
tables first,=20
before accessing the local tables. This works well for cases like <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004880">Example&nbsp;2-14</A>,=20
where the driving table is the remote table. However, if the driving =
table is=20
the local table, then there might not be any selective way of accessing =
the=20
remote table without first accessing the local tables. In such cases, =
you might=20
need to provide appropriate hints to avoid performance problems.</P><A=20
name=3D1012775></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-16 How the=20
CBO Evaluates a Query with a Local Driving Table and a Nested Loops=20
Hint</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1004928></A>SELECT =
/*+USE_NL(c h) */ c.customer_name, h.order_number
<A name=3D1004929></A>  FROM ra_customers c, so_headers_all@oe h
<A name=3D1004930></A> WHERE c.customer_id =3D h.customer_id
<A name=3D1004931></A>   AND c.customer_name LIKE :b1;
<A name=3D1004932></A>
<A name=3D1004933></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004934></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004935></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004936></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1004937></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1004938></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_N1
<A name=3D1004939></A>  REMOTE
</PRE>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1008539></A><STRONG>Remote Database Query Obtained from the =
Library=20
  Cache</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1004942></A>SELECT /*+ USE_NL("H") */ =
"ORDER_NUMBER","CUSTOMER_ID"
<A name=3D1004943></A>  FROM "SO_HEADERS_ALL" "H" WHERE =
:1=3D"CUSTOMER_ID"
<A name=3D1004944></A>FILTER;
<A name=3D1004945></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1004946></A>
<P class=3DBP>The construct used by the optimizer is applying a filter =
condition=20
to filter out rows; this filter could not be applied when the table was=20
accessed. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008670">Example&nbsp;2-17</A>=20
uses no filter.</P><A name=3D1008670></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-17 Querying=20
a Remote Table Using No Filter</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1006419></A>SELECT h.order_number
<A name=3D1004949></A>  FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004950></A> WHERE h.open_flag =3D 'Y'
<A name=3D1004951></A>   AND attribute1 IS NOT NULL;
<A name=3D1004952></A>
<A name=3D1004953></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004954></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004955></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004956></A> TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004957></A>  INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N2
<A name=3D1004958></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1008683></A>
<P class=3DBP>Besides the conditions used in the access path, a table =
might have=20
additional conditions to filter rows when the table is visited. =
Conditions that=20
get applied when the table is accessed, like <CODE>attribute1</CODE>=20
<CODE>IS</CODE> <CODE>NOT</CODE> <CODE>NULL</CODE>, do not show up as=20
<CODE>FILTER</CODE>. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004959">Example&nbsp;2-18</A>=20
and <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004975">Example&nbsp;2-19</A>=20
illustrate such conditions.</P><A name=3D1010192></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004959">Example&nbsp;2-18</A>=20
shows a query with a <CODE>GROUP</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> condition that =
creates a=20
filter.</P><A name=3D1004959></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-18 Querying=20
with a Filter Created by a GROUP BY Condition</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1004960></A>SELECT h.order_number, count(*)
<A name=3D1004961></A>  FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004962></A> WHERE h.open_flag =3D 'Y'
<A name=3D1004963></A>   AND attribute1 IS NOT NULL
<A name=3D1004964></A>GROUP BY h.order_number
<A name=3D1004965></A>HAVING COUNT(*) =3D 1 =DF Filter condition;
<A name=3D1004966></A>
<A name=3D1004967></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004968></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004969></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004970></A> FILTER
<A name=3D1004971></A>  SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1004972></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004973></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N2
<A name=3D1004974></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010189></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1004975">Example&nbsp;2-19</A>=20
shows a query with a subquery that creates a filter.</P><A =
name=3D1004975></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-19 Querying=20
with a Filter Created by a Subquery</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1004977></A>SELECT h.order_number
<A name=3D1004978></A>  FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1004979></A> WHERE h.open_flag =3D 'Y'
<A name=3D1004980></A>   AND EXISTS (SELECT null FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1004981></A>               WHERE l.header_id =3D h.header_id
<A name=3D1004982></A>               AND l.revenue_amount &gt; 10000);
<A name=3D1006424></A>
<A name=3D1006425></A>Plan
<A name=3D1004985></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1004986></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1004987></A> FILTER
<A name=3D1004988></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1004989></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N2
<A name=3D1004990></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1004991></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1010165></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010166></A>
<P class=3DBP>In this example, for every row meeting the condition of =
the outer=20
query, the correlated <CODE>EXISTS</CODE> subquery is executed. If a row =
meeting=20
the condition is found in the <CODE>so_lines_all</CODE> table, then the =
row from=20
<CODE>so_headers_all</CODE> is returned.</P><A =
name=3D1004993></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1004993"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Executes Distributed Statements</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1004999></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer chooses execution plans for SQL statements =
that access=20
data on remote databases in much the same way that it chooses execution =
plans=20
for statements that access only local data:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005000></A>If all the tables =
accessed by a=20
  SQL statement are located together on the same remote database, then =
Oracle=20
  sends the SQL statement to that remote database. The remote Oracle =
instance=20
  executes the statement and sends only the results back to the local =
database.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005001></A>If a SQL statement =
accesses tables=20
  that are located on different databases, then Oracle decomposes the =
statement=20
  into individual fragments, each of which accesses tables on a single =
database.=20
  Oracle then sends each fragment to the database that it accesses. The =
remote=20
  Oracle instance for each of these databases executes its fragment and =
returns=20
  the results to the local database, where the local Oracle instance can =
perform=20
  any additional processing that the statement requires. </LI></UL><A=20
name=3D1005003></A>
<P class=3DBP>When choosing a cost-based execution plan for a =
distributed=20
statement, the optimizer considers the available indexes on remote =
databases,=20
just as it considers indexes on the local database. The optimizer also =
considers=20
statistics on remote databases for the CBO. Furthermore, the optimizer =
considers=20
the location of data when estimating the cost of accessing it. For =
example, a=20
full scan of a remote table has a greater estimated cost than a full =
scan of an=20
identical local table.</P><A name=3D1005004></A>
<P class=3DBP>For a rule-based execution plan, the optimizer does not =
consider=20
indexes on remote&nbsp;tables.</P><A name=3D1005012></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1005007></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH><STRONG=20
      class=3DBold>See Also</STRONG>:</STRONG></FONT> <A =
name=3D1005011></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/sql_1016.htm#22018">Chapter&nbsp;6,=20
      "Optimizing SQL Statements"</A> for more information on tuning =
distributed=20
      queries</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D1005014></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005014"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Executes Sort Operations</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005015></A>
<P class=3DBP>Sort operations result when users specify some operation =
that=20
requires a sort. Commonly encountered operations include the =
following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005019></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005037">SORT=20
  UNIQUE</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005023></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005091">SORT=20
  AGGREGATE</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005027></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008958">SORT=20
  GROUP BY</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005031></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005118">SORT=20
  JOIN</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005035></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005137">SORT=20
  ORDER BY</A> </LI></UL><A name=3D1005037></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005037"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>SORT=20
UNIQUE</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1005038></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>UNIQUE</CODE> occurs if a user =
specifies a=20
<CODE>DISTINCT</CODE> clause (<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005039">Example&nbsp;2-20</A>)=20
or if an operation requires unique values for the next step (<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005051">Example&nbsp;2-21</A>).</P><A=20
name=3D1005039></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-20 DISTINCT=20
Clause Causing SORT UNIQUE</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005041></A>SELECT DISTINCT last_name, first_name
<A name=3D1005042></A> FROM per_all_people_f
<A name=3D1005043></A> WHERE full_name LIKE :b1;
<A name=3D1005044></A>
<A name=3D1005045></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005046></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005047></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005048></A> SORT UNIQUE
<A name=3D1005049></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID PER_ALL_PEOPLE_F
<A name=3D1005050></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN PER_PEOPLE_F_N54
</PRE><A name=3D1005051></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-21 IN=20
Subquery Causing SORT UNIQUE</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1005052></A>
<P class=3DBP>SORT UNIQUE provides the outer query with a unique list of =

<CODE>header_id</CODE>s. The plan shows that the <CODE>IN</CODE> =
subquery has=20
been un-nested and transformed into VW_NSO_1.</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005053></A>SELECT c.customer_name, h.order_number
<A name=3D1005054></A>  FROM ra_customers c, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005055></A> WHERE c.customer_id =3D h.customer_id
<A name=3D1005056></A>   AND h.header_id in
<A name=3D1005057></A>     (SELECT l.header_id FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1005058></A>      WHERE l.inventory_item_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1005059></A>      AND ordered_quantity &gt; 10);
<A name=3D1006430></A>
<A name=3D1006431></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005062></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005063></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005064></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005065></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005066></A>   VIEW  VW_NSO_1
<A name=3D1005067></A>    SORT UNIQUE
<A name=3D1005068></A>     TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005069></A>      INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N5
<A name=3D1005070></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005071></A>    INDEX UNIQUE SCAN SO_HEADERS_U1
<A name=3D1005072></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1005073></A>   INDEX UNIQUE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_U1
<A name=3D1008811></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1008804></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the optimizer can guarantee (with unique keys) that =
duplicate=20
values will not be passed, then a sort can be avoided, as in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005074">Example&nbsp;2-22</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1005074></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-22 IN=20
Subquery That Does Not Cause SORT UNIQUE</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005076></A>UPDATE so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1005077></A>   SET line_status =3D 'HOLD'
<A name=3D1005078></A> WHERE l.header_id IN
<A name=3D1005079></A>     ( SELECT h.header_id FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005080></A>        WHERE h.customer_id =3D :b1);
<A name=3D1005081></A>
<A name=3D1005082></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005083></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005084></A>UPDATE STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005085></A> UPDATE  SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005086></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005087></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005088></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N1
<A name=3D1005089></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
</PRE><A name=3D1005091></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005091"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>SORT=20
AGGREGATE</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1005092></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>AGGREGATE</CODE> does not actually =
involve a=20
sort. It is used when aggregates are being computed across the whole set =
of=20
rows, as shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008830">Example&nbsp;2-23</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1008830></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-23 Query=20
Causing SORT AGGREGATE</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005093></A>SELECT SUM(l.revenue_amount)
<A name=3D1005094></A>  FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1005095></A> WHERE l.header_id =3D :b1;
<A name=3D1008793></A>
<A name=3D1008794></A>Plan
<A name=3D1008795></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005099></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005100></A> SORT AGGREGATE
<A name=3D1005101></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1008956></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
</PRE><A name=3D1008958></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1008958"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>SORT GROUP=20
BY</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1008959></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>GROUP</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> is =
used when=20
aggregates are being computed for different groups in the data. The sort =
is=20
required to separate the rows into different groups, as shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1008993">Example&nbsp;2-24</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1008993></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-24 Query=20
That Causes SORT GROUP BY</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005106></A>SELECT created_by, SUM(l.revenue_amount)
<A name=3D1005107></A>  FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1005108></A> WHERE header_id &gt; :b1
<A name=3D1005109></A>GROUP BY created_by;
<A name=3D1005110></A>
<A name=3D1005111></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005112></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005113></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005114></A> SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1005115></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005116></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
</PRE><A name=3D1005118></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005118"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>SORT=20
JOIN</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1005119></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>JOIN</CODE> happens during a=20
<CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>MERGE</CODE> <CODE>JOIN</CODE>, if the rows need =
to be=20
sorted by the join key, as shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009052">Example&nbsp;2-25</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1009052></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-25 Query=20
Causing SORT JOIN</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005120></A>SELECT SUM(l.revenue_amount), l2.creation_date
<A name=3D1005121></A>  FROM so_lines_all l, so_lines_All l2
<A name=3D1005122></A> WHERE l.creation_date &lt; l2.creation_date
<A name=3D1005123></A>   AND l.header_id &lt;&gt; l2.header_id
<A name=3D1005124></A>GROUP BY l2.creation_date, l2.line_id;
<A name=3D1005125></A>
<A name=3D1005126></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005127></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005128></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005129></A> SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1005130></A>  MERGE JOIN
<A name=3D1005131></A>   SORT JOIN
<A name=3D1005132></A>    TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005133></A>   FILTER
<A name=3D1005134></A>    SORT JOIN
<A name=3D1005135></A>     TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
</PRE><A name=3D1005137></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005137"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>SORT ORDER=20
BY</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1005138></A>
<P class=3DBP><CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>ORDER</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> is =
required=20
when the statement specifies an <CODE>ORDER</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE> that =
cannot=20
be satisfied by one of the indexes, as shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009086">Example&nbsp;2-26</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1009086></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-26 Query=20
Causing SORT ORDER BY</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005139></A>SELECT h.order_number
<A name=3D1005140></A>  FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005141></A> WHERE h.customer_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1005142></A> ORDER BY h.creation_date DESC;
<A name=3D1005143></A>
<A name=3D1005144></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005145></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005146></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005147></A> SORT ORDER BY
<A name=3D1005148></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005149></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N1
</PRE><A name=3D1005152></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005152"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Executes Views</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1005153></A>
<P class=3DBP>Either of the following can result in creation of a view =
by the=20
CBO:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005154></A>A complex view has =
not been=20
  decomposed (<A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005156">Example&nbsp;2-27</A>).=20

  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005155></A>A temporary or =
inline view is=20
  being used (Examples <A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005174">2-28</A>=20
  and <A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005199">2-29</A>).=20
  </LI></UL><A name=3D1005156></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-27 Query=20
Causing a View</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005157></A>SELECT order_id
<A name=3D1005158></A>FROM orders
<A name=3D1005159></A> WHERE customer_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1005160></A>   AND revenue &gt; :b2;
<A name=3D1005161></A>
<A name=3D1005162></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005163></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005164></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005165></A> VIEW  ORDERS
<A name=3D1005166></A>  FILTER
<A name=3D1005167></A>   SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1005168></A>    NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005169></A>     TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005170></A>      INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N1
<A name=3D1005171></A>     TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005172></A>      INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N1
<A name=3D1009158></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1009151></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005174">Example&nbsp;2-28</A>,=20
a view is created because the <CODE>IN</CODE> subquery requires a=20
<CODE>SORT</CODE> <CODE>UNIQUE</CODE> on the values being selected. This =
view=20
would be unnecessary if the columns being selected were unique, not =
requiring a=20
sort.</P><A name=3D1005174></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-28 IN=20
Subquery Causing a View</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005178></A>SELECT c.customer_name, h.order_number
<A name=3D1005179></A>  FROM ra_customers c, so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005180></A> WHERE c.customer_id =3D h.customer_id
<A name=3D1005181></A>   AND h.header_id IN
<A name=3D1005182></A>     (SELECT l.header_id FROM so_lines_all l
<A name=3D1005183></A>      WHERE l.inventory_item_id =3D :b1
<A name=3D1005184></A>      AND ordered_quantity &gt; 10);
<A name=3D1005186></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005187></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005188></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005189></A> NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005190></A>  NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005191></A>   VIEW  VW_NSO_1
<A name=3D1005192></A>    SORT UNIQUE
<A name=3D1005193></A>     TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_LINES_ALL
<A name=3D1005194></A>      INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_LINES_N5
<A name=3D1005195></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005196></A>    INDEX UNIQUE SCAN SO_HEADERS_U1
<A name=3D1005197></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1005198></A>   INDEX UNIQUE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_U1
<A name=3D1009174></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1009171></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005199">Example&nbsp;2-29</A>=20
examines the distribution of orders and revenue by the number of lines =
in each=20
order. The CBO uses temporary inline views to do the double =
grouping.</P><A=20
name=3D1005199></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-29 Inline=20
Query Causing a View</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005201></A>SELECT COUNT(*) "Orders", cnt "Lines", sum(rev) =
"Revenue"
<A name=3D1005202></A>FROM (SELECT header_id, COUNT(*) cnt, =
SUM(revenue_amount) rev
<A name=3D1005203></A>        FROM so_lines_all
<A name=3D1005204></A>       GROUP BY header_id)
<A name=3D1005205></A>GROUP BY cnt;
<A name=3D1006444></A>
<A name=3D1006445></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005208></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005209></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005210></A> SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1005211></A>  VIEW
<A name=3D1005212></A>   SORT GROUP BY
<A name=3D1005213></A>    TABLE ACCESS FULL SO_LINES_ALL
</PRE><A name=3D1005216></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005216"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates Constants</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1005219></A>
<P class=3DBP>Computation of constants is performed only once, when the =
statement=20
is optimized, rather than each time the statement is executed.</P><A=20
name=3D1005220></A>
<P class=3DBP>For example, the following conditions all test for monthly =
salaries=20
greater than 2000:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005221></A>salary &gt; =
24000/12
<A name=3D1005223></A>salary &gt; 2000
<A name=3D1005225></A>salary*12 &gt; 24000
<A name=3D1005226></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005227></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a SQL statement contains the first condition, then the =
optimizer=20
simplifies it into the second condition.</P><A name=3D1005232></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D1005230></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005231></A>
      <P class=3DNB>The optimizer does not simplify expressions across =
comparison=20
      operators: In the preceding examples, the optimizer does not =
simplify the=20
      third expression into the second. For this reason, application =
developers=20
      write conditions that compare columns with constants whenever =
possible,=20
      rather than conditions with expressions involving columns.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A =
name=3D1005234></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005234"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the UNION and UNION ALL =
Operators</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A=20
name=3D1005235></A>
<P class=3DBP>This operator is useful for combining <CODE>OR</CODE> =
clauses into=20
one compound statement or for breaking up a complex statement into a =
compound=20
statement containing simpler select statements that are easier to =
optimize and=20
understand.</P><A name=3D1005236></A>
<P class=3DBP>As with concatenation, you do not want to duplicate =
expensive=20
operations by using <CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE>.</P><A =
name=3D1005237></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005237"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>When the=20
Optimizer Uses UNION or UNION ALL</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D1005238></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer uses <CODE>UNION</CODE> or =
<CODE>UNION</CODE>=20
<CODE>ALL</CODE> when the SQL statement contains <CODE>UNION</CODE> or=20
<CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE> clauses.</P><A name=3D1005240></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005241">Example&nbsp;2-30</A>=20
shows a query without a <CODE>UNION</CODE> clause. The query finds =
customers who=20
are new or have open orders.</P><A name=3D1005241></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-30 Query=20
Without UNION Clause</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005242></A>SELECT c.customer_name, c.creation_date
<A name=3D1005243></A>  FROM ra_customers c
<A name=3D1005244></A> WHERE c.creation_date &gt; SYSDATE - 30
<A name=3D1005245></A>    OR customer_id IN
<A name=3D1005246></A>     (SELECT customer_id FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005247></A>        WHERE h.open_flag =3D 'Y');
<A name=3D1005248></A>
<A name=3D1005249></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005250></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005251></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005252></A> FILTER
<A name=3D1005253></A>  TABLE ACCESS FULL RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1005254></A>  TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005255></A>   INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N1
<A name=3D1005256></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005257></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because the driving conditions come from different tables, =
you=20
cannot execute the query effectively in a single statement.</P><A=20
name=3D1005258></A>
<P class=3DBP>With a <CODE>UNION</CODE> clause, you can break the query =
into two=20
statements:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005259></A>New customers=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005260></A>Customers with open =
orders=20
</LI></UL><A name=3D1005261></A>
<P class=3DBP>These two statements can be optimized easily. Because you =
do not=20
want duplicates (some customers meet both criteria), use =
<CODE>UNION</CODE>,=20
which eliminates duplicates by using a sort. If you use two disjoint =
sets, then=20
you can use <CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE>, eliminating the sort. =
The query=20
from <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005241">Example&nbsp;2-30</A>=20
is shown using <CODE>UNION</CODE> in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005262">Example&nbsp;2-31</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1005262></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-31 Query=20
Using the UNION Clause</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1006448></A>SELECT c.customer_name, c.creation_date
<A name=3D1005264></A>  FROM ra_customers c
<A name=3D1005265></A> WHERE c.creation_date &gt; SYSDATE - 30
<A name=3D1005266></A>UNION ALL =20
<A name=3D1005267></A>SELECT c.customer_name, c.creation_date
<A name=3D1005268></A>  FROM ra_customers c
<A name=3D1005269></A> WHERE customer_id IN
<A name=3D1005270></A>     (SELECT customer_id FROM so_headers_all h
<A name=3D1005271></A>        WHERE h.open_flag =3D 'Y');
<A name=3D1005272></A>
<A name=3D1005273></A>Plan
<A name=3D1005274></A>--------------------------------------------------
<A name=3D1005275></A>SELECT STATEMENT
<A name=3D1005276></A> SORT UNIQUE
<A name=3D1005277></A>  UNION-ALL
<A name=3D1005278></A>   TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1005279></A>    INDEX RANGE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_N2
<A name=3D1005280></A>   NESTED LOOPS
<A name=3D1005281></A>    TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID SO_HEADERS_ALL
<A name=3D1005282></A>     INDEX RANGE SCAN SO_HEADERS_N2
<A name=3D1005283></A>    TABLE ACCESS BY INDEX ROWID RA_CUSTOMERS
<A name=3D1005284></A>     INDEX UNIQUE SCAN RA_CUSTOMERS_U1
</PRE><A name=3D1005285></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005285"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>UNION and=20
UNION ALL Hints</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A name=3D1005286></A>
<P class=3DBP>There are no hints for this operation.</P><A =
name=3D1005289></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005289"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the LIKE Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005290></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer simplifies conditions that use the =
<CODE>LIKE</CODE>=20
comparison operator to compare an expression with no wildcard characters =
into an=20
equivalent condition that uses an equality operator instead.</P><A=20
name=3D1010320></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following example, the optimizer simplifies the =
first=20
condition into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1010322></A>last_name LIKE 'SMITH'
<A name=3D1010354></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010355></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1010374></A>last_name =3D 'SMITH'
<A name=3D1005294></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005295></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer can simplify these expressions only when the =

comparison involves variable-length datatypes. For example, if=20
<CODE>last_name</CODE> was of type <CODE>CHAR</CODE>(10), then the =
optimizer=20
cannot transform the <CODE>LIKE</CODE> operation into an equality =
operation due=20
to the equality operator following blank-padded semantics and =
<CODE>LIKE</CODE>=20
not following blank-padded semantics.</P><A =
name=3D1005298></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005298"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the IN Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005299></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer expands a condition that uses the =
<CODE>IN</CODE>=20
comparison operator to an equivalent condition that uses equality =
comparison=20
operators and <CODE>OR</CODE> logical operators.</P><A =
name=3D1010457></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following example, the optimizer expands the first =
condition=20
into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005300></A>last_name IN =
('SMITH', 'KING', 'JONES')=20
<A name=3D1010386></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010387></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005302></A>last_name =3D 'SMITH' OR last_name =3D 'KING' OR =
last_name =3D 'JONES'=20
<A name=3D1010393></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005313></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1005305></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005312></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005841">"How=20
      the CBO Merges an IN Subquery"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005841"></A></P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D1005317></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005317"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the ANY or SOME Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005318></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer expands a condition that uses the =
<CODE>ANY</CODE> or=20
<CODE>SOME</CODE> comparison operator followed by a parenthesized list =
of values=20
into an equivalent condition that uses equality comparison operators and =

<CODE>OR</CODE> logical operators.</P><A name=3D1010461></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following example, the optimizer expands the first =
condition=20
into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005319></A>salary &gt; =
ANY (:first_sal, :second_sal)=20
<A name=3D1010395></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010396></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005321></A>salary &gt; :first_sal OR salary &gt; :second_sal=20
<A name=3D1005322></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005323></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms a condition that uses the =
<CODE>ANY</CODE>=20
or <CODE>SOME</CODE> operator followed by a subquery into a condition =
containing=20
the <CODE>EXISTS</CODE> operator and a correlated subquery.</P><A=20
name=3D1010490></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following example, the optimizer transforms the =
first=20
condition into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1010491></A>x =
&gt; ANY (SELECT salary
<A name=3D1005325></A>    FROM employees
<A name=3D1005326></A>    WHERE job_id =3D 'IT_PROG')
<A name=3D1010401></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010402></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1009288></A>EXISTS (SELECT salary
<A name=3D1005329></A>    FROM employees
<A name=3D1005330></A>    WHERE job_id =3D 'IT_PROG'
<A name=3D1005331></A>      AND x &gt; salary)
</PRE><A name=3D1005334></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005334"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the ALL Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005335></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer expands a condition that uses the =
<CODE>ALL</CODE>=20
comparison operator followed by a parenthesized list of values into an=20
equivalent condition that uses equality comparison operators and=20
<CODE>AND</CODE> logical operators.</P><A name=3D1010469></A>
<P class=3DBP>In the following example, the optimizer expands the first =
condition=20
into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005336></A>salary &gt; =
ALL (:first_sal, :second_sal)
<A name=3D1010407></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010408></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005338></A>salary &gt; :first_sal AND salary &gt; :second_sal
<A name=3D1005339></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005340></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms a condition that uses the =
<CODE>ALL</CODE>=20
comparison operator followed by a subquery into an equivalent condition =
that=20
uses the <CODE>ANY</CODE> comparison operator and a complementary =
comparison=20
operator. In the following example, the optimizer transforms the first =
condition=20
into the second:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005341></A>x &gt; ALL =
(SELECT salary
<A name=3D1005342></A>    FROM employees
<A name=3D1005343></A>    WHERE department_id =3D 50)
<A name=3D1010413></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010414></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005345></A>NOT (x &lt;=3D ANY (SELECT salary
<A name=3D1005346></A>      FROM employees
<A name=3D1005347></A>      WHERE department_id =3D 50) )
<A name=3D1005348></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005349></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer then further transforms the second query =
into the=20
following query using the rule for transforming conditions with the=20
<CODE>ANY</CODE> comparison operator, followed by a correlated =
subquery:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005350></A>NOT EXISTS (SELECT =
salary
<A name=3D1005351></A>      FROM employees
<A name=3D1005352></A>      WHERE department_id =3D 50
<A name=3D1005353></A>        AND x &lt;=3D salary)
</PRE><A name=3D1005356></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005356"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the BETWEEN Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005357></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer always replaces a condition that uses the=20
<CODE>BETWEEN</CODE> comparison operator with an equivalent condition =
that uses=20
the &gt;=3D and &lt;=3D comparison operators. In the following example, =
the=20
optimizer replaces the first condition with the second:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005358></A>salary BETWEEN 2000 AND 3000
<A name=3D1010419></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010420></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005360></A>salary &gt;=3D 2000 AND salary &lt;=3D 3000=20
</PRE><A name=3D1005363></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005363"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates the NOT Operator</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1009954></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer simplifies a condition to eliminate the=20
<CODE>NOT</CODE> logical operator. The simplification involves removing =
the=20
<CODE>NOT</CODE> logical operator and replacing a comparison operator =
with its=20
opposite comparison operator. In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009956">Example&nbsp;2-32</A>,=20
the optimizer simplifies the first condition into the second one:</P><A=20
name=3D1009956></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-32 How the=20
CBO Evaluates the NOT Operator</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCEM><A =
name=3D1006451></A>NOT department_id =3D (SELECT department_id FROM =
employees WHERE last_name =3D 'Taylor')
<A name=3D1010425></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010426></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCEM><A =
name=3D1005367></A>department_id &lt;&gt; (SELECT department_id FROM =
employees WHERE last_name =3D 'Taylor')
<A name=3D1005368></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005369></A>
<P class=3DBP>Often, a condition containing the <CODE>NOT</CODE> logical =
operator=20
can be written in many different ways. The optimizer attempts to =
transform such=20
a condition so that the subconditions negated by <CODE>NOT</CODE>s are =
as simple=20
as possible, even if the resulting condition contains more =
<CODE>NOT</CODE>s. In=20
<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009981">Example&nbsp;2-33</A>,=20
the optimizer simplifies the first condition into the second, and then =
into the=20
third.</P><A name=3D1009981></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-33 How the=20
CBO Simplifies a NOT Statement</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005370></A>NOT (salary &lt; 1000 OR commission_pct IS NULL)
<A name=3D1005371></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010432></A>
<P class=3DBP>is transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005372></A>NOT salary &lt; 1000 AND commission_pct IS NOT NULL
<A name=3D1005373></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010441></A>
<P class=3DBP>which is further transformed into</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005374></A>salary &gt;=3D 1000 AND commission_pct IS NOT NULL
</PRE><A name=3D1005377></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005377"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates Transitivity</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1005378></A>
<P class=3DBP>If two conditions in the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause involve =
a common=20
column, then the optimizer sometimes can infer a third condition, using =
the=20
transitivity principle. The optimizer can then use the inferred =
condition to=20
optimize the statement. The inferred condition can make available an =
index=20
access path that was not made available by the original =
conditions.</P><A=20
name=3D1005383></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D1005381></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005382></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Transitivity is used only by the CBO.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D1005384></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider a <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause containing two =
conditions of=20
these forms:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005385></A>WHERE column1 =
comp_oper constant
<A name=3D1005386></A>  AND column1 =3D column2
<A name=3D1005387></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005388></A>
<P class=3DBP>In this case, the optimizer infers the condition:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005389></A>column2 comp_oper constant
<A name=3D1005390></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005391></A>
<P class=3DBP>where:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D1011562></A><CODE>comp_oper</CODE> is any of=20
  the comparison operators <CODE>=3D</CODE>, <CODE>!=3D</CODE>, =
<CODE>^=3D</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>&lt;</CODE>, <CODE>&lt;&gt;</CODE>, <CODE>&gt;</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>&lt;=3D</CODE>, or <CODE>&gt;=3D</CODE>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A =
name=3D1011567></A><CODE>constant</CODE> is any=20
  constant expression involving operators, SQL functions, literals, bind =

  variables, and correlation variables </LI></UL><A name=3D1009358></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005402">Example&nbsp;2-34</A>,=20
the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause contains two conditions, each of which =
uses the=20
<CODE>employees</CODE>.<CODE>department_id</CODE> column.</P><A=20
name=3D1005402></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-34 Query=20
Causing Transitivity Evaluation</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005404></A>SELECT *
<A name=3D1011956></A>  FROM employees, departments
<A name=3D1011957></A>  WHERE employees.department_id =3D 20
<A name=3D1011958></A>    AND employees.department_id =3D =
departments.department_id;
<A name=3D1005408></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005409></A>
<P class=3DBP>Using transitivity, the optimizer infers the following=20
condition:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005410></A>departments.department_id =3D 20
<A name=3D1005411></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005412></A>
<P class=3DBP>If an index exists on the=20
<CODE>departments</CODE>.<CODE>department_id</CODE> column, then this =
condition=20
makes available access paths using that index.</P><A name=3D1005416></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer only infers conditions that relate columns =
to constant=20
expressions, rather than columns to other columns. Consider a =
<CODE>WHERE</CODE>=20
clause containing two conditions of these forms:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005417></A>WHERE column1 comp_oper column3
<A name=3D1005418></A>  AND column1 =3D column2
<A name=3D1009412></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005420></A>
<P class=3DBP>In this case, the optimizer does not infer the condition,=20
<CODE>column2</CODE> <CODE>comp_oper</CODE> <CODE>column3</CODE>.</P><A=20
name=3D1005424></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005424"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Optimizes Common Subexpressions</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005425></A>
<P class=3DBP>Common subexpression optimization is an optimization =
heuristic that=20
identifies, removes, and collects common subexpressions from disjunctive =

(<CODE>OR</CODE>) branches of a query. In most cases, it reduces the =
number of=20
joins to be performed. This optimization is enabled with the=20
<CODE>OPTIMIZER_FEATURES_ENABLE</CODE> initialization parameter.</P><A=20
name=3D1005427></A>
<P class=3DBP>A query is considered valid for common subexpression =
optimization if=20
its <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause is in the following form:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005428></A>The top-level is a =
disjunction (a=20
  list of <CODE>OR</CODE>ed logs).=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005429></A>Each disjunct is =
either a simple=20
  predicate or a conjunction (a list of <CODE>AND</CODE>ed logs).=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005430></A>Each conjunct is =
either a simple=20
  predicate or a disjunction of simple predicates. (A predicate is =
considered=20
  simple if it does not contain <CODE>AND</CODE> or <CODE>OR</CODE>.)=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005431></A>The expression =
appears in all the=20
  disjunctive branches of the query. </LI></UL><A =
name=3D1005432></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005432"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Examples of=20
Common Subexpression Optimization</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D1005433></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009457">Example&nbsp;2-35</A>=20
finds names of employees who work in a department located in London and =
who make=20
more than 60K or who are accountants. The query contains common =
subexpressions=20
in its two disjunctive branches.</P><A name=3D1009457></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-35=20
Optimization of Common Subexpressions in Two OR =
Branches</EM></FONT></H4>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009673></A><STRONG>Initial Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005434></A>SELECT employees.last_name
<A name=3D1005435></A> FROM employees E, departments D
<A name=3D1005436></A> WHERE (D.department_id =3D E.department_id AND =
E.job_id =3D 'AC_ACCOUNT' AND=20
D.location =3D 2400)
<A name=3D1011917></A>  OR
<A name=3D1005438></A>  E.department_id =3D D.department_id AND E.salary =
&gt; 60000 AND D.location =3D 2400);
<A name=3D1005439></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005440></A>
<P class=3DBP>The elimination of the common subexpressions transforms =
this query=20
into the following query, reducing the number of joins from two to =
one.</P>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009677></A><STRONG>Optimized Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005441></A>SELECT employees.last_name=20
<A name=3D1009484></A>FROM employees E, departments D
<A name=3D1005442></A>WHERE (D.department_id =3D E.department_id AND =
D.location =3D 2400)
<A name=3D1005443></A>   AND (E.job_id =3D 'AC_ACCOUNT' OR E.salary &gt; =
60000);
<A name=3D1005444></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005445></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009470">Example&nbsp;2-36</A>=20
contains common subexpression in three disjunctive branches.</P><A=20
name=3D1009470></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-36=20
Optimization of Common Subexpressions in Three OR =
Branches</EM></FONT></H4>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009669></A><STRONG>Initial Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005446></A>SELECT SUM (l_extendedprice* =
(1 - l_discount))
<A name=3D1005447></A>FROM PARTS, LINEITEM
<A name=3D1005448></A>WHERE (p_partkey =3D l_partkey
<A name=3D1005449></A>        AND p_brand =3D 'Brand#12'
<A name=3D1005450></A>        AND p_container IN ('SM CASE', 'SM BOX', =
'SM PACK', 'SM PKG')
<A name=3D1005451></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 1 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 1 + 10
<A name=3D1005452></A>        AND p_size &gt;=3D 1 AND p_size &lt;=3D 5
<A name=3D1005453></A>        AND l_shipmode IN ('AIR', 'REG AIR')
<A name=3D1005454></A>        AND l_shipinstruct =3D 'DELIVER IN =
PERSON')
<A name=3D1005455></A>    OR (l_partkey =3D p_partkey)
<A name=3D1005456></A>        AND p_brand =3D 'Brand#23'
<A name=3D1005457></A>        AND p_container IN ('MED BAG', 'MED BOX', =
'MED PKG', 'MED PACK')
<A name=3D1005458></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 10 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 10 + 10
<A name=3D1005459></A>        AND p_size &gt;=3D 1 AND p_size &lt;=3D 10 =
AND p_size BETWEEN 1 AND 10
<A name=3D1005460></A>        AND l_shipmode IN ('AIR', 'REG AIR')
<A name=3D1005461></A>        AND l_shipinstruct =3D 'DELIVER IN =
PERSON')
<A name=3D1005462></A>    OR (p_partkey =3D l_partkey
<A name=3D1005463></A>        AND p_brand =3D 'Brand#34'
<A name=3D1005464></A>        AND p_container IN ('LG CASE', 'LG BOX', =
'LG PACK', 'LG PKG')
<A name=3D1005465></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 20 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 20 + 10
<A name=3D1005466></A>        AND p_size &gt;=3D 1 AND p_size &lt;=3D 15
<A name=3D1005467></A>        AND l_shipmode IN ('AIR', 'REG AIR')
<A name=3D1005468></A>        AND l_shipinstruct =3D 'DELIVER IN =
PERSON');
<A name=3D1005469></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005470></A>
<P class=3DBP>This query is transformed by common subexpression =
optimization to=20
the following query, reducing the number joins from three to one.</P>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009663></A><STRONG>Optimized Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005471></A>SELECT SUM (l_extendedprice* =
(1 - l_discount))
<A name=3D1005472></A>FROM PARTS, LINEITEM
<A name=3D1005473></A>WHERE (p_partkey =3D l_partkey  /* these are the =
four common subexpressions */
<A name=3D1005474></A>    AND p_size &gt;=3D 1
<A name=3D1005475></A>    AND l_shipmode IN ('AIR', 'REG AIR')
<A name=3D1005476></A>    AND l_shipinstruct =3D 'DELIVER IN PERSON')
<A name=3D1005477></A>    AND
<A name=3D1005478></A>        ((p_brand =3D 'Brand#12'
<A name=3D1005479></A>        AND p_container IN  ( 'SM CASE', 'SM BOX', =
'SM PACK', 'SM PKG')
<A name=3D1005480></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 1 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 1 + 10
<A name=3D1005481></A>        AND p_size &lt;=3D 5)
<A name=3D1005482></A>    OR (p_brand =3D 'Brand#23'
<A name=3D1005483></A>        AND p_container IN ('MED BAG', 'MED BOX', =
'MED PKG', 'MED PACK')
<A name=3D1005484></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 10 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 10 + 10
<A name=3D1005485></A>        AND p_size &lt;=3D 10)
<A name=3D1005486></A>    OR (p_brand =3D 'Brand#34'
<A name=3D1005487></A>        AND p_container IN ('LG CASE', 'LG BOX', =
'LG PACK', 'LG PKG')
<A name=3D1005488></A>        AND l_quantity &gt;=3D 20 AND l_quantity =
&lt;=3D 20 + 10
<A name=3D1005489></A>AND p_size &lt;=3D 15));
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005493></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005493"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Evaluates DETERMINISTIC Functions</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1005495></A>
<P class=3DBP>In some cases, the optimizer can use a previously =
calculated value=20
rather than executing a user-written function. This is only safe for =
functions=20
that behave in a restricted manner. The function must return the same =
output=20
return value for any given set of input argument values.</P><A =
name=3D1005496></A>
<P class=3DBP>The function's result must not differ because of =
differences in the=20
content of package variables or the database, or session parameters such =
as the=20
globalization support parameters. Furthermore, if the function is =
redefined in=20
the future, then its output return value must be the same as that =
calculated=20
with the prior definition for any given set of input argument values. =
Finally,=20
there must be no meaningful side effects to using a precalculated value =
instead=20
of executing the function again.</P><A name=3D1005497></A>
<P class=3DBP>The creator of a function can promise to the Oracle server =
that the=20
function behaves according to these restrictions by using the keyword=20
<CODE>DETERMINISTIC</CODE> when declaring the function with a=20
<CODE>CREATE</CODE> <CODE>FUNCTION</CODE> statement or in a =
<CODE>CREATE</CODE>=20
<CODE>PACKAGE</CODE> or <CODE>CREATE</CODE> <CODE>TYPE</CODE> statement. =
The=20
server does not attempt to verify this declaration--even a function that =

obviously manipulates the database or package variables can be declared=20
<CODE>DETERMINISTIC</CODE>. It is the programmer's responsibility to use =
this=20
keyword only when appropriate.</P><A name=3D1005498></A>
<P class=3DBP>Calls to a <CODE>DETERMINISTIC</CODE> function might be =
replaced by=20
the use of an already calculated value when the function is called =
multiple=20
times within the same query, or if there is a function-based index or a=20
materialized view defined that includes a relevant call to the =
function.</P><A=20
name=3D1005527></A>
