<H2 class=3DH1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the=20
Optimizer Transforms SQL Statements</FONT></H2><!--/TOC=3Dh1--><A=20
name=3D1005538></A>
<P class=3DBP>SQL is a very flexible query language; there are often =
many=20
statements you could use to achieve the same goal. Sometimes, the =
optimizer=20
(Query Transformer) transforms one such statement into another that =
achieves the=20
same goal if the second statement can be executed more =
efficiently.</P><A=20
name=3D1005539></A>
<P class=3DBP>This section discusses the following topics:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005543></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005572">How=20
  the CBO Transforms ORs into Compound Queries</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005547></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005654">How=20
  the CBO Unnests Subqueries</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005551></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009807">How=20
  the CBO Merges Views</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005555></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005864">How=20
  the CBO Pushes Predicates</A>=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005559></A><A=20
  =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006050">How=20
  the CBO Executes Compound Queries</A>=20
  <P><A name=3D1005570></A></P>
  <DIV align=3Dcenter>
  <TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
  cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
  summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
    <TBODY>
    <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
      <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1005562></A><FONT=20
        face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
        Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005569></A>
        <P class=3DNB><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473">"Understanding=20
        Joins"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473"></A>=20
        for additional information about optimizing statements that =
contain=20
        joins, semi-joins, or =
anti-joins</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV></LI></UL><A=20
name=3D1005572></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005572"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Transforms ORs into Compound Queries</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A=20
name=3D1005576></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a query contains a <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause with =
multiple=20
conditions combined with <CODE>OR</CODE> operators, then the optimizer=20
transforms it into an equivalent compound query that uses the =
<CODE>UNION</CODE>=20
<CODE>ALL</CODE> set operator, if this makes the query execute more=20
efficiently:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005578></A>If each condition =
individually=20
  makes an index access path available, then the optimizer can make the=20
  transformation. The optimizer chooses an execution plan for the =
resulting=20
  statement that accesses the table multiple times using the different =
indexes=20
  and then puts the results together.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005579></A>If any condition =
requires a full=20
  table scan because it does not make an index available, then the =
optimizer=20
  does not transform the statement. The optimizer chooses a full table =
scan to=20
  execute the statement, and Oracle tests each row in the table to =
determine=20
  whether it satisfies any of the conditions.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1009607></A>For statements that =
use the CBO,=20
  the optimizer might use statistics to determine whether to make the=20
  transformation, by estimating and then comparing execution costs of =
the=20
  original statement and the resulting statement.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005581></A>The CBO does not use =
the=20
  <CODE>OR</CODE> transformation for <CODE>IN</CODE>-lists or =
<CODE>OR</CODE>s=20
  on the same column; instead, it uses the <CODE>INLIST</CODE> iterator=20
  operator.=20
  <P><A name=3D1005598></A></P>
  <DIV align=3Dcenter>
  <TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
  cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
  summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
    <TBODY>
    <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
      <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1005584></A><FONT=20
        face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
        Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005591></A>
        <P class=3DNB><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893">"Understanding=20
        Access Paths for the RBO"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/rbo.htm#38893"></A>=20
        and <A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#48061">"How=20
        the CBO Chooses an Access Path"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#48061"></A>=20
        for information on access paths and how indexes make them=20
      available</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV></LI></UL><A =
name=3D1009645></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005599">Example&nbsp;2-37</A>=20
shows how an <CODE>OR</CODE> query is transformed into a compound query. =
In this=20
example, the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause contains two conditions combined =
with an=20
<CODE>OR</CODE> operator.</P><A name=3D1005599></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-37=20
Transforming an OR Query into a Compound Query</EM></FONT></H4>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1005600></A><STRONG>Initial Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005601></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005602></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005603></A>  WHERE job_id =3D 'ST_CLERK'=20
<A name=3D1005604></A>    OR department_id =3D 50;=20
<A name=3D1005605></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005606></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there are indexes on both the <CODE>job_id</CODE> and=20
<CODE>department_id</CODE> columns, then the optimizer might transform =
this=20
query into the following equivalent query:</P>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1012892></A><STRONG>Optimized Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1007375></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005608></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005609></A>  WHERE job_id =3D 'ST_CLERK'=20
<A name=3D1005611></A>UNION ALL=20
<A name=3D1005612></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005613></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005614></A>  WHERE department_id =3D 50=20
<A name=3D1005615></A>    AND job_id &lt;&gt; 'ST_CLERK';=20
<A name=3D1005616></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005617></A>
<P class=3DBP>When the CBO is deciding whether to make a transformation, =
the=20
optimizer compares the cost of executing the original query using a full =
table=20
scan with that of executing the resulting query.</P><A =
name=3D1012461></A>
<P class=3DBP>The execution plan for the transformed statement might =
look like the=20
illustration in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006519">Figure&nbsp;2-1</A>.=20
The shaded boxes indicate steps that physically retrieve data and the =
clear=20
boxes indicate steps that operate on data returned from the previous =
step.</P><A=20
name=3D1006519></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-1 Execution=20
Plan for a Transformed Query Containing OR</EM></FONT></H4><A =
name=3D1005629><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf199.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf199.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf199.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf199.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1012312></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute the transformed query, Oracle performs the =
steps in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006519">Figure&nbsp;2-1</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1005631></A>Steps 3 and 5 =
scan the=20
  indexes on the <CODE>job_id</CODE> and <CODE>department_id</CODE> =
columns=20
  using the conditions of the component queries. These steps obtain =
rowids of=20
  the rows that satisfy the component queries.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1005632></A>Steps 2 and 4 =
use the rowids=20
  from steps 3 and 5 to locate the rows that satisfy each component =
query.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1005633></A>Step 1 puts =
together the row=20
  sources returned by steps 2 and 4. </LI></OL><A name=3D1005634></A>
<P class=3DBP>If either the <CODE>job_id</CODE> column or the=20
<CODE>department_id</CODE> column is not indexed, then the optimizer =
does not=20
even consider the transformation, because the resulting compound query =
would=20
require a full table scan to execute one of its component queries. =
Executing the=20
compound query with a full table scan in addition to an index scan could =
not=20
possibly be faster than executing the original query with a full table=20
scan.</P><A name=3D1005635></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009723">Example&nbsp;2-38</A>=20
shows the kind of query that is not transformed. The query in this =
example=20
assumes that there is an index on the <CODE>last_name</CODE> column =
only:</P><A=20
name=3D1009723></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-38 Query=20
Containing OR That Is Not Transformed</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005637></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005638></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005639></A>  WHERE last_name =3D 'Smith'=20
<A name=3D1005640></A>    OR salary &gt; commission_pct*100000;=20
<A name=3D1005641></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005642></A>
<P class=3DBP>Transforming this query would result in the compound query =
in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1012789">Example&nbsp;2-39</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1012789></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-39 Compound=20
Query Containing OR That Is Transformed</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005643></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005644></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005645></A>  WHERE last_name =3D 'Smith'=20
<A name=3D1005647></A>UNION ALL=20
<A name=3D1005648></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005649></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005650></A>  WHERE salary &gt; commission_pct*100000;=20
<A name=3D1005651></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005652></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because the condition in the <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause of =
the second=20
component query (<CODE>salary</CODE> &gt; <CODE>commission_pct</CODE>) =
does not=20
make an index available, the compound query requires a full table scan. =
For this=20
reason, the optimizer does not make the transformation, but chooses a =
full table=20
scan to execute the original statement.</P><A =
name=3D1005654></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005654"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Unnests Subqueries</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1005660></A>
<P class=3DBP>To optimize a complex statement, the optimizer chooses to =
do one of=20
the following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005661></A>Transform the =
complex statement=20
  into an equivalent join statement, and then optimize the join =
statement=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005662></A>Optimize the complex =
statement as=20
  it is </LI></UL><A name=3D1005663></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms a complex statement into a join =
statement=20
whenever the resulting join statement is guaranteed to return exactly =
the same=20
rows as the complex statement. This transformation allows Oracle to =
execute the=20
statement by taking advantage of join optimizer techniques.</P><A=20
name=3D1005670></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009759">Example&nbsp;2-40</A>=20
shows how the optimizer uses a join to unnest a subquery. The complex =
statement=20
in this example selects all rows from the <CODE>orders</CODE> table for =
which=20
owners appear in the <CODE>customers</CODE> table.</P><A =
name=3D1009759></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-40 How the=20
CBO Unnests Subqueries</EM></FONT></H4>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009770></A><STRONG>Initial Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005671></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005672></A>  FROM orders=20
<A name=3D1005673></A>  WHERE customer_id IN=20
<A name=3D1005674></A>    (SELECT customer_id FROM customers);=20
<A name=3D1005675></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1005678></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the <CODE>customer_id</CODE> of the =
<CODE>customers</CODE> table=20
is a primary key or has a <CODE>UNIQUE</CODE> constraint, then the =
optimizer can=20
transform the complex query into the following join statement that is =
guaranteed=20
to return the same data:</P>
<DL class=3DA1>
  <DT class=3DA1><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
size=3D-1><A=20
  name=3D1009778></A><STRONG>Optimized Query</STRONG></FONT>=20
  <DD><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1012422></A>SELECT orders.*=20
<A name=3D1012423></A>  FROM orders, customers=20
<A name=3D1005679></A>  WHERE orders.customer_id =3D =
customers.customer_id;=20
<A name=3D1005682></A>
</PRE></DD></DL><A name=3D1009869></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs a nested-loops =
join=20
operation. The execution plan for this statement might look like <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005690">Figure&nbsp;2-2</A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1005690></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-2 Execution=20
Plan for a Nested Loops Join</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1005694><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf200.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf200.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf200.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf200.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1005707></A><A=20
name=3D1005708></A>
<P class=3DBP>If the optimizer cannot transform a complex statement into =
a join=20
statement, then it chooses execution plans for the parent statement and =
the=20
subquery as though they were separate statements. Oracle then executes =
the=20
subquery and uses the rows returned to execute the parent query.</P><A=20
name=3D1005709></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1009890">Example&nbsp;2-41</A>=20
shows the kind of statement is not transformed. The complex statement in =
this=20
example returns all rows from the <CODE>customers</CODE> table that have =
credit=20
limits greater than the average credit limit.</P><A name=3D1009890></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-41 Complex=20
Statement That Is Not Transformed</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1012441></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1012442></A>  FROM customers=20
<A name=3D1012443></A>  WHERE credit_limit &gt;=20
<A name=3D1005710></A>    (SELECT AVG(credit_limit) FROM customers);=20
<A name=3D1005714></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005715></A>
<P class=3DBP>No join statement can perform the function of this =
statement, so the=20
optimizer does not transform the statement.</P><A name=3D1009805></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNote dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to format =
a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNote>
    <TD class=3DNote>
      <HR>
      <A name=3D1009803></A><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, =
sans-serif"><STRONG=20
      class=3DNH>Note:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1009804></A>
      <P class=3DNB>Complex queries whose subqueries contain aggregate =
functions=20
      such as <CODE>AVG</CODE> cannot be transformed into join =
statements.</P>
      <HR>
    </TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A name=3D1009827></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
  <TBODY>
  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1009839></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1009846></A>
      <P class=3DNB><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473">"Understanding=20
      Joins"</A><A=20
      =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/optimops.htm#39473"></A>=20
      for information on nested loops =
joins</P></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D1009807></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1009807"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Merges Views</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1009810></A>
<P class=3DBP>To merge the view's query into a referencing query block =
in the=20
accessing statement, the optimizer replaces the name of the view with =
the names=20
of its base tables in the query block and adds the condition of the =
view's=20
query's <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause to the accessing query block's=20
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause.</P><A name=3D1005728></A>
<P class=3DBP>This optimization applies to <STRONG=20
class=3DGlossaryTerm>select-project-join</STRONG> views, which are views =
that=20
contain only selections, projections, and joins. This means that these =
views do=20
not contain items such as set operators, aggregate functions,=20
<CODE>DISTINCT</CODE>, <CODE>GROUP</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE>, and=20
<CODE>CONNECT</CODE> <CODE>BY</CODE>. See <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005755">"Mergeable=20
and Nonmergeable Views"</A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005755"></A>.</P><A=20
name=3D1010516></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005735">Example&nbsp;2-42</A>=20
shows how the CBO merges views. The view in this example is of all =
employees who=20
work in department 10:</P><A name=3D1005735></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-42 How the=20
CBO Merges Views</EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005737></A>CREATE VIEW emp_10=20
<A name=3D1005738></A>  AS SELECT employee_id, last_name, job_id, =
manager_id, hire_date, salary,=20
commission_pct, department_id=20
<A name=3D1005739></A>    FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005740></A>    WHERE department_id =3D 10;=20
<A name=3D1005741></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005742></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following query accesses the view. The query selects =
the Ids=20
greater than 170 of employees who work in department 10:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005743></A>SELECT employee_id=20
<A name=3D1005744></A>  FROM emp_10=20
<A name=3D1005745></A>  WHERE employee_id &gt; 170;=20
<A name=3D1005746></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005747></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms the query into the following =
query that=20
accesses the view's base table:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005748></A>SELECT employee_id=20
<A name=3D1005749></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005750></A>  WHERE department_id =3D 10=20
<A name=3D1005751></A>    AND employee_id &gt; 170;=20
<A name=3D1005752></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005753></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there are indexes on the <CODE>department_id</CODE> or=20
<CODE>employee_id</CODE> columns, then the resulting <CODE>WHERE</CODE> =
clause=20
makes them available.</P><A name=3D1005755></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"1005755"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>Mergeable=20
and Nonmergeable Views</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A name=3D1005756></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer can merge a view into a referencing query =
block when=20
the view has one or more base tables, provided the view does not contain =
any of=20
the following:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005761></A>Set operators =
(<CODE>UNION</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE>, <CODE>INTERSECT</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>MINUS</CODE>)=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005763></A>A =
<CODE>CONNECT</CODE>=20
  <CODE>BY</CODE> clause=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005767></A>A =
<CODE>ROWNUM</CODE> pseudocolumn=20

  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005768></A>Aggregate functions=20
  (<CODE>AVG</CODE>, <CODE>COUNT</CODE>, <CODE>MAX</CODE>, =
<CODE>MIN</CODE>,=20
  <CODE>SUM</CODE>) in the select list </LI></UL><A name=3D1005769></A>
<P class=3DBP>When a view contains one of the following structures, it =
can be=20
merged into a referencing query block only if <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005784">Complex=20
View Merging</A> is enabled:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005770></A>A <CODE>GROUP</CODE> =

  <CODE>BY</CODE> clause=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005771></A>A =
<CODE>DISTINCT</CODE> operator=20
  in the select list </LI></UL><A name=3D1005772></A>
<P class=3DBP>View merging is not possible for a view that has multiple =
base=20
tables, if it is on the right side of an outer join. However, if a view =
on the=20
right side of an outer join has only one base table, then the optimizer =
can use=20
complex view merging, even if an expression in the view can return a =
nonnull=20
value for a <CODE>NULL</CODE>.</P><A name=3D1011245></A>
<P class=3DBP>If a query has a <CODE>CURSOR</CODE> expression, then no =
view=20
merging will take place, even for views which would normally be =
mergeable. An=20
example is the following:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1011248></A>CREATE VIEW emp_v AS
<A name=3D1011273></A>       SELECT last_name,employee_id FROM =
employees;=20
<A name=3D1011250></A>SELECT CURSOR(select * from sys.dual), last_name, =
employee_id from emp_v;=20
<A name=3D1011251></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1011243></A>
<P class=3DBP>This query will not merge the <CODE>emp_v</CODE> =
view.</P><A=20
name=3D1005783></A>
<DIV align=3Dcenter>
<TABLE class=3DNoteAlso dir=3Dltr title=3D"This is a layout table to =
format a note"=20
cellSpacing=3D0 cellPadding=3D0 width=3D"80%"=20
summary=3D"This is a layout table to format a note" border=3D0>
