  <TR class=3DNoteAlso>
    <TD class=3DNoteAlso><A name=3D1005814></A><FONT=20
      face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><STRONG class=3DNH>See=20
      Also:</STRONG></FONT> <A name=3D1005818></A>
      <UL class=3DNL>
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1010575></A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005864">"How=20
        the CBO Pushes Predicates"</A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005864"></A>=20
        for an alternate approach=20
        <LI class=3DNL type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1010540></A><A=20
        =
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/hintsref.htm#8326">Chapter&nbsp;5,=20
        "Optimizer Hints"</A> for details about the <CODE>MERGE</CODE> =
and=20
        <CODE>NO_MERGE</CODE> hints =
</LI></UL></TD></TR></TBODY></TABLE></DIV><A=20
name=3D1005820></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-43 How the=20
CBO Merges a View with a GROUP BY Clause</EM></FONT></H4><A =
name=3D1005821></A>
<P class=3DBP>The view <CODE>avg_salary_view</CODE> contains the average =
salaries=20
for each department:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005822></A>CREATE =
VIEW avg_salary_view AS=20
<A name=3D1005823></A>  SELECT department_id, AVG(salary) AS =
avg_sal_dept,=20
<A name=3D1005824></A>    FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005825></A>    GROUP BY department_id;
<A name=3D1011241></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005826></A>
<P class=3DBP>If complex view merging is enabled, then the optimizer can =
transform=20
the following query, which finds the average salaries of departments in=20
London:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005827></A>SELECT =
departments.location_id, avg_sal_dept=20
<A name=3D1005828></A>  FROM departments, avg_salary_view=20
<A name=3D1005829></A>  WHERE departments.department_id =3D =
avg_salary_view.department_id=20
<A name=3D1005830></A>    AND departments.location_id =3D 2400;=20
<A name=3D1005831></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005832></A>
<P class=3DBP>into the following query:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005833></A>SELECT departments.loc, AVG(salary)=20
<A name=3D1005834></A>  FROM departments, employees=20
<A name=3D1005835></A>  WHERE departments.department_id =3D =
employees.department_id=20
<A name=3D1005836></A>    AND departments.location_id =3D 2400=20
<A name=3D1005837></A>  GROUP BY departments.rowid, =
departments.location_id;=20
<A name=3D1005838></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005839></A>
<P class=3DBP>The transformed query accesses the view's base table, =
selecting only=20
the rows of employees who work in London and grouping them by =
department.</P><A=20
name=3D1005841></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-44 How the=20
CBO Merges an IN Subquery</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1005843></A>
<P class=3DBP>Complex merging can be used for an <CODE>IN</CODE> clause =
with a=20
noncorrelated subquery, as well as for views. The view=20
<CODE>min_salary_view</CODE> contains the minimum salaries for each=20
department:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1011013></A>CREATE VIEW =
min_salary_view AS
<A name=3D1011014></A>SELECT department_id, MIN(salary) min_sal
<A name=3D1011015></A>  FROM employees
<A name=3D1011016></A> GROUP BY department_id;
<A name=3D1005848></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1011011></A>
<P class=3DBP>If complex merging is enabled, then the optimizer can =
transform the=20
following query, which finds all employees who earn the minimum salary =
for their=20
department in London:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1011032></A>SELECT =
employees.last_name, employees.salary
<A name=3D1011033></A>  FROM employees, departments
<A name=3D1011034></A> WHERE (employees.department_id, employees.salary) =
IN
<A name=3D1011035></A>         (select department_id, min_sal from =
min_salary_view)
<A name=3D1011036></A>   AND employees.department_id =3D =
departments.department_id
<A name=3D1011037></A>   AND departments.location_id =3D 2400;
<A name=3D1011045></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005855></A>
<P class=3DBP>into the following query (where <CODE>e1</CODE> and =
<CODE>e2</CODE>=20
represent the <CODE>employees</CODE> table as it is referenced in the =
accessing=20
query block and the view's query block, respectively):</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005856></A>SELECT e1.last_name, e1.salary=20
<A name=3D1005857></A>  FROM employees e1, departments, employees e2=20
<A name=3D1005858></A> WHERE e1.department_id =3D =
departments.department_id=20
<A name=3D1005859></A>   AND departments.location_id =3D 2400
<A name=3D1005860></A>   AND e1.department_id =3D e2.department_id=20
<A name=3D1005861></A> GROUP BY e1.rowid, departments.rowid, =
e1.last_name, e1.salary=20
<A name=3D1005862></A>HAVING e1.salary =3D MIN(e2.salary);=20
</PRE><A name=3D1005864></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1005864"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Pushes Predicates</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A name=3D1010614></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer can transform a query block that accesses a=20
nonmergeable view by pushing the query block's predicates inside the =
view's=20
query. Examples <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005867">2-45</A>=20
and <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010652">2-46</A>=20
illustrate this process.</P><A name=3D1010672></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005867">Example&nbsp;2-45</A>,=20
the <CODE>two_emp_tables</CODE> view is the union of two employee =
tables. The=20
view is defined with a compound query that uses the <CODE>UNION</CODE> =
set=20
operator.</P><A name=3D1005867></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-45 How the=20
CBO Pushes Predicates - the UNION Set Operator</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005870></A>CREATE VIEW two_emp_tables=20
<A name=3D1005871></A>  (employee_id, last_name, job_id, manager_id, =
hire_date, salary,=20
<A name=3D1012823></A>                                   commission_pct, =
department_id) AS=20
<A name=3D1005872></A>  SELECT employee_id, last_name, job_id, =
manager_id, hire_date, salary,=20
commission_pct, department_id=20
<A name=3D1005873></A>    FROM emp1=20
<A name=3D1005874></A>   UNION =20
<A name=3D1005875></A>  SELECT employee_id, last_name, job_id, =
manager_id, hire_date,=20
<A name=3D1012824></A>                          salary, commission_pct, =
department_id=20
<A name=3D1005876></A>    FROM emp2;=20
<A name=3D1005877></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005878></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following query accesses the view. The query selects =
the IDs and=20
names of all employees in either table who work in department =
50:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005879></A>SELECT employee_id, =
last_name=20
<A name=3D1005880></A>  FROM two_emp_tables=20
<A name=3D1005881></A> WHERE department_id =3D 50;=20
<A name=3D1005882></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005883></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because the view is defined as a compound query, the =
optimizer=20
cannot merge the view's query into the accessing query block. Instead, =
the=20
optimizer can transform the accessing statement by pushing its =
predicate, the=20
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause condition (<CODE>department_id</CODE> =3D 50), =
into the=20
view's compound query.</P><A name=3D1005885></A>
<P class=3DBP>The resulting statement looks like the following:</P><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005886></A>SELECT employee_id, last_name=20
<A name=3D1005887></A>  FROM ( SELECT employee_id, last_name, job_id, =
manager_id, hire_date,=20
<A name=3D1012825></A>                                salary, =
commission_pct, department_id=20
<A name=3D1005888></A>           FROM emp1=20
<A name=3D1005889></A>          WHERE department_id =3D 50=20
<A name=3D1005890></A>          UNION=20
<A name=3D1005891></A>         SELECT employee_id, last_name, job_id, =
manager_id, hire_date,=20
<A name=3D1012826></A>                                 salary, =
commission_pct, department_id=20
<A name=3D1005892></A>           FROM emp2=20
<A name=3D1005893></A>          WHERE department_id =3D 50 );=20
<A name=3D1005894></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005895></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there is an index on the <CODE>department_id</CODE> =
column, then=20
the resulting <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clauses make it available.</P><A=20
name=3D1005901></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005903">Figure&nbsp;2-3</A>=20
shows the execution plan of the resulting statement.</P><A =
name=3D1005903></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-3 Accessing=20
a View Defined with the UNION Set Operator</EM></FONT></H4><A =
name=3D1005907><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf201.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf201.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf201.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf201.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1005908></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005903">Figure&nbsp;2-3</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1005909></A>Steps 5 and 6 =
perform full=20
  scans of the <CODE>emp1</CODE> and <CODE>emp2</CODE> tables.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1005910></A>Step 4 =
performs a=20
  <CODE>UNION</CODE>-<CODE>ALL</CODE> operation returning all rows =
returned=20
  by&nbsp;either step 5 or step 6, including all copies of duplicates.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1005911></A>Step 3 sorts =
the result of=20
  step 4, eliminating duplicate rows.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D4><A name=3D1005912></A>Step 2 =
extracts the desired=20
  columns from the result of step 3.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D5><A name=3D1005913></A>Step 1 =
indicates that the=20
  view's query was not merged into the accessing&nbsp;query. =
</LI></OL><A=20
name=3D1010685></A>
<P class=3DBP>In <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010652">Example&nbsp;2-46</A>,=20
the view <CODE>emp_group_by_deptno</CODE> contains the department =
number,=20
average salary, minimum salary, and maximum salary of all departments =
that have=20
employees.</P><A name=3D1010652></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-46 How the=20
CBO Pushes Predicates - the GROUP BY Clause</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005915></A>CREATE VIEW emp_group_by_deptno=20
<A name=3D1005916></A>  AS SELECT department_id,=20
<A name=3D1005917></A>        AVG(salary) avg_sal,=20
<A name=3D1005918></A>        MIN(salary) min_sal,=20
<A name=3D1005919></A>        MAX(salary) max_sal=20
<A name=3D1005920></A>       FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005921></A>      GROUP BY department_id;=20
<A name=3D1005922></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005923></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following query selects the average, minimum, and =
maximum=20
salaries of department 50 from the <CODE>emp_group_by_deptno</CODE> =
view:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005924></A>SELECT *=20
<A name=3D1005925></A>  FROM emp_group_by_deptno=20
<A name=3D1005926></A> WHERE department_id =3D 50;=20
<A name=3D1005927></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005929></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms the statement by pushing its =
predicate (the=20
<CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause condition) into the view's query. The =
resulting=20
statement looks like the following:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1005930></A>SELECT department_id,=20
<A name=3D1005931></A>   AVG(salary) avg_sal,=20
<A name=3D1005932></A>   MIN(salary) min_sal,=20
<A name=3D1005933></A>   MAX(salary) max_sal,=20
<A name=3D1005934></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005935></A> WHERE department_id =3D 50=20
<A name=3D1005936></A> GROUP BY department_id;=20
<A name=3D1005937></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005938></A>
<P class=3DBP>If there is an index on the <CODE>department_id</CODE> =
column, then=20
the resulting <CODE>WHERE</CODE> clause makes it available. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005945">Figure&nbsp;2-4</A>=20
shows the execution plan for the resulting statement. The execution plan =
uses an=20
index on the <CODE>department_id</CODE> column.</P><A =
name=3D1005945></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-4 Accessing=20
a View Defined with a GROUP BY Clause</EM></FONT></H4><A =
name=3D1005949><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf202.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf202.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf202.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf202.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1005950></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005945">Figure&nbsp;2-4</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1005951></A>Step 4 =
performs a range scan=20
  on the index <CODE>emp_department_ix</CODE> (an index on the=20
  <CODE>department_id</CODE> column of the <CODE>employees</CODE> table) =
to=20
  retrieve the rowids of all rows in the <CODE>employees</CODE> table =
with a=20
  <CODE>department_id</CODE> value of 50.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1005952></A>Step 3 =
accesses the=20
  <CODE>employees</CODE> table using the rowids retrieved by step 4.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1005953></A>Step 2 sorts =
the rows=20
  returned by step 3 to calculate the average, minimum, and maximum=20
  <CODE>salary</CODE> values.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D4><A name=3D1005954></A>Step 1 =
indicates that the=20
  view's query was not merged into the accessing query. </LI></OL><A=20
name=3D1005955></A><!--TOC=3Dh4-"1005955"-->
<H5 class=3DH4><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Applies an Aggregate Function to the View</FONT></H5><!--/TOC=3Dh4--><A=20
name=3D1005957></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer can transform a query that contains an =
aggregate=20
function (<CODE>AVG</CODE>, <CODE>COUNT</CODE>, <CODE>MAX</CODE>,=20
<CODE>MIN</CODE>, <CODE>SUM</CODE>) by applying the function to the =
view's=20
query.</P><A name=3D1010743></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005959">Figure&nbsp;2-47</A>=20
accesses the <CODE>emp_group_by_deptno</CODE> view defined in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010652">Figure&nbsp;2-46</A>.=20
The query derives the averages for the average department salary, the =
minimum=20
department salary, and the maximum department salary from the employee=20
table.</P><A name=3D1005959></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-47 How the=20
CBO Applies an Aggregate Function to a View</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1005961></A>SELECT AVG(avg_sal), AVG(min_sal), =
AVG(max_sal)=20
<A name=3D1005962></A>  FROM emp_group_by_deptno;=20
<A name=3D1005963></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005964></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer transforms this statement by applying the=20
<CODE>AVG</CODE> aggregate function to the select list of the view's =
query:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1005965></A>SELECT AVG(AVG(salary)), =
AVG(MIN(salary)), AVG(MAX(salary))=20
<A name=3D1005966></A>  FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1005967></A> GROUP BY department_id;=20
<A name=3D1005968></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1005974></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005976">Figure&nbsp;2-5</A>=20
shows the execution plan of the resulting statement.</P><A =
name=3D1005976></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-5 Applying=20
Aggregate Functions to a View Defined with GROUP BY =
Clause</EM></FONT></H4><A=20
name=3D1005980><IMG alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf203.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf203.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf203.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf203.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1005981></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1005976">Figure&nbsp;2-5</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1005982></A>Step 4 =
performs a full scan=20
  of the <CODE>employees</CODE> table.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1005983></A>Step 3 sorts =
the rows=20
  returned by step 4 into groups based on their =
<CODE>department_id</CODE>=20
  values and calculates the average, minimum, and maximum =
<CODE>salary</CODE>=20
  value of each group.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1005984></A>Step 2 =
indicates that the=20
  view's query was not merged into the accessing query.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D4><A name=3D1005985></A>Step 1 =
calculates the=20
  averages of the values returned by step 2. </LI></OL><A =
name=3D1005986></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005986"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Executes Views in Outer Joins</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A =
name=3D1005987></A>
<P class=3DBP>For a view that is on the right side of an outer join, the =
optimizer=20
can use one of two methods, depending on how many base tables the view=20
accesses:</P>
<UL class=3DLB1>
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005992></A>If the view has only =
one base=20
  table, then the optimizer can use view merging.=20
  <LI class=3DLB1 type=3Ddisc><A name=3D1005993></A>If the view has =
multiple base=20
  tables, then the optimizer can push the join predicate into&nbsp;the =
view.=20
  </LI></UL><A name=3D1005995></A><!--TOC=3Dh3-"1005995"-->
<H4 class=3DH3><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Accesses the View's Rows with the Original =
Statement</FONT></H4><!--/TOC=3Dh3--><A=20
name=3D1005997></A>
<P class=3DBP>The optimizer cannot transform all statements that access =
views into=20
equivalent statements that access base table(s). For example, if a query =

accesses a <CODE>ROWNUM</CODE> pseudocolumn in a view, then the view =
cannot be=20
merged into the query, and the query's predicate cannot be pushed into =
the=20
view.</P><A name=3D1006001></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute a statement that cannot be transformed into one =
that=20
accesses base tables, Oracle issues the view's query, collects the =
resulting set=20
of rows, and then accesses this set of rows with the original statement =
as=20
though it were a table. <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010759">Example&nbsp;2-48</A>=20
illustrates this process.</P><A name=3D1010759></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-48 How the=20
CBO Accesses the View's Rows</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1010760></A>
<P class=3DBP>Consider the <CODE>emp_group_by_deptno</CODE> view defined =
in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010652">Figure&nbsp;2-46</A>:</P><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1010761></A>CREATE VIEW emp_group_by_deptno=20
<A name=3D1010762></A>  AS SELECT department_id,=20
<A name=3D1006006></A>        AVG(salary) avg_sal,
<A name=3D1006007></A>        MIN(salary) min_sal,=20
<A name=3D1006008></A>        MAX(salary) max_sal=20
<A name=3D1006009></A>       FROM employees=20
<A name=3D1006010></A>      GROUP BY department_id;=20
<A name=3D1006011></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010802></A>
<P class=3DBP>The following query accesses this view. The query joins =
the average,=20
minimum, and maximum salaries from each department represented in the =
view to=20
the name and location of the department in the <CODE>departments</CODE>=20
table.</P><PRE class=3DCE><A name=3D1006013></A>SELECT =
emp_group_by_deptno.department_id, avg_sal, min_sal,
<A name=3D1006014></A>       max_sal, department_name, location_id
<A name=3D1006015></A>  FROM emp_group_by_deptno, departments=20
<A name=3D1006016></A> WHERE emp_group_by_deptno.department_id =3D =
departments.department_id;
<A name=3D1012897></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010787></A>
<P class=3DBP>Because there is no equivalent statement that accesses =
only base=20
tables, the optimizer cannot transform this statement. Instead, the =
optimizer=20
chooses an execution plan that issues the view's query and then uses the =

resulting set of rows as it would the rows resulting from a table =
access.</P><A=20
name=3D1006029></A>
