<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006457">Figure&nbsp;2-6</A>=20
shows the execution plan for this statement.</P><A name=3D1006457></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-6 Joining a=20
View Defined with a GROUP BY Clause to a Table</EM></FONT></H4><A=20
name=3D1006041><IMG alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf204.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf204.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf204.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf204.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1012329></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006457">Figure&nbsp;2-6</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1006043></A>Step 4 =
performs a full scan=20
  of the <CODE>employees</CODE> table.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1006044></A>Step 3 sorts =
the results of=20
  step 4 and calculates the average, minimum, and maximum =
<CODE>salary</CODE>=20
  values selected by the query for the <CODE>emp_group_by_deptno</CODE> =
view.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1006045></A>Step 2 used =
the data from the=20
  previous two steps for a view.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D4><A name=3D1006046></A>For each row =
returned by step=20
  2, step 6 uses the <CODE>department_id</CODE> value to perform a =
unique scan=20
  of the <CODE>dept_id_pk</CODE> index.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D5><A name=3D1006047></A>Step 5 uses =
each rowid=20
  returned by step 6 to locate the row in the <CODE>departments</CODE> =
table=20
  with the matching <CODE>department_id</CODE> value.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D6><A name=3D1006048></A>Oracle =
combines each row=20
  returned by step 2 with the matching row returned by step 5 and =
returns the=20
  result. </LI></OL><A name=3D1006050></A><!--TOC=3Dh2-"1006050"-->
<H3 class=3DH2><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" =
color=3D#330099>How the CBO=20
Executes Compound Queries</FONT></H3><!--/TOC=3Dh2--><A =
name=3D1006052></A>
<P class=3DBP>To choose the execution plan for a compound query, the =
optimizer=20
chooses an execution plan for each of its component queries, and then =
combines=20
the resulting row sources with the union, intersection, or minus =
operation,=20
depending on the set operator used in the compound query. Examples <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010807">2-49</A>,=20
<A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010918">2-50</A>=20
and <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010943">2-51</A>=20
illustrate the process.</P><A name=3D1010899></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010807">Example&nbsp;2-49</A>=20
uses the <CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE> operator to select all =
occurrences=20
of all parts in either the <CODE>orders1</CODE> table or the=20
<CODE>orders2</CODE> table.</P><A name=3D1010807></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-49 How the=20
CBO Executes Compound Queries with <STRONG><CODE>UNION</CODE></STRONG>=20
<STRONG><CODE>ALL</CODE></STRONG></EM></FONT></H4><PRE class=3DCE><A =
name=3D1006060></A>SELECT part FROM orders1=20
<A name=3D1006061></A> UNION ALL=20
<A name=3D1006062></A>SELECT part FROM orders2;=20
<A name=3D1010833></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010840></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006064">Figure&nbsp;2-7</A>=20
shows the execution plan for this statement:</P><A name=3D1006064></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-7 Compound=20
Query with UNION ALL Set Operator</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1006068><IMG =

alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf205.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf205.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf205.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf205.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1006069></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006064">Figure&nbsp;2-7</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1006070></A>Steps 2 and 3 =
perform full=20
  table scans on the <CODE>orders1</CODE> and <CODE>orders2</CODE> =
tables.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1006071></A>Step 1 =
performs a=20
  <CODE>UNION</CODE>-<CODE>ALL</CODE> operation returning all rows that =
are=20
  returned by either step 2 or step 3 including all copies of =
duplicates.=20
</LI></OL><A name=3D1006077></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010918">Example&nbsp;2-50</A>=20
uses the <CODE>UNION</CODE> operator to select all parts that appear in =
either=20
the <CODE>orders1</CODE> or <CODE>orders2</CODE> table.</P><A =
name=3D1010918></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-50 How the=20
CBO Executes Compound Queries with UNION</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1010919></A>SELECT part FROM orders1=20
<A name=3D1010920></A> UNION=20
<A name=3D1010921></A>SELECT part FROM orders2;=20
<A name=3D1010924></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010889></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006083">Figure&nbsp;2-8</A>=20
shows the execution plan for this statement:</P><A name=3D1006083></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-8 Compound=20
Query with UNION Set Operator</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1006087><IMG=20
alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf206.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf206.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf206.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf206.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1006088></A>
<P class=3DBP>This execution plan is identical to the one for the=20
<CODE>UNION</CODE> <CODE>ALL</CODE> operator shown in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006064">Figure&nbsp;2-7</A>,=20
except that in this case, Oracle uses the <CODE>SORT</CODE> operation to =

eliminate the duplicates returned by the <CODE>UNION</CODE> =
<CODE>ALL</CODE>=20
operation.</P><A name=3D1010949></A>
<P class=3DBP>The query in <A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1010943">Example&nbsp;2-51</A>=20
uses the <CODE>INTERSECT</CODE> operator to select only those parts that =
appear=20
in both the <CODE>orders1</CODE> and <CODE>orders2</CODE> tables.</P><A=20
name=3D1010943></A>
<H4 class=3DET><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Example =
2-51 How the=20
CBO Executes Compound Queries with INTERSECT</EM></FONT></H4><PRE =
class=3DCE><A name=3D1006102></A>SELECT part FROM orders1=20
<A name=3D1006103></A>INTERSECT=20
<A name=3D1006104></A>SELECT part FROM orders2;=20
<A name=3D1010976></A>
</PRE><A name=3D1010973></A>
<P class=3DBP><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006106">Figure&nbsp;2-9</A>=20
shows the execution plan for this statement:</P><A name=3D1006106></A>
<H4 class=3DFT><FONT face=3D"Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif"><EM>Figure =
2-9 Compound=20
Query with INTERSECT Set Operator</EM></FONT></H4><A name=3D1006110><IMG =

alt=3D"Text description of pfgrf207.gif follows"=20
src=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a9653=
3/pfgrf207.gif"></A><A=20
href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/img_text/pfgrf207.htm"><BR>Text=20
description of the illustration pfgrf207.gif</A><BR><BR><A =
name=3D1006111></A>
<P class=3DBP>To execute this statement, Oracle performs the steps in <A =

href=3D"http://www.cise.ufl.edu/help/database/oracle-docs/server.920/a965=
33/opt_ops.htm#1006106">Figure&nbsp;2-9</A>=20
in the following order:</P>
<OL class=3DLN1 type=3D1>
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D1><A name=3D1006112></A>Steps 3 and 5 =
perform full=20
  table scans of the <CODE>orders1</CODE> and <CODE>orders2</CODE> =
tables.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D2><A name=3D1006113></A>Steps 2 and 4 =
sort the=20
  results of steps 3 and 5, eliminating duplicates in each row source.=20
  <LI class=3DLN1 type=3D1 value=3D3><A name=3D1006114></A>Step 1 =
performs an=20
  <CODE>INTERSECTION</CODE> operation that returns only rows that are =
returned=20
  by both steps 2 and 4. </LI></OL><!-- Start Footer --></DIV>
